-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/402e312f-d269-4b9c-a4f8-6c723136f9c9
-- title:
--   Theorem 2.3 — no online algorithm exceeds $(1/2+\varepsilon)L$ except with probability $e^{-(\varepsilon^2/2)L\log(np)}$
-- statement:
--   Fix $\kappa\in(0,1)$ and $\varepsilon>0$, and let $L$ be given by (1). There is a constant $C=C(\varepsilon,\kappa)>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $n$, all $C/n\le p\le1-\kappa$ and every online algorithm as in Definition 2.2, run on $G\sim G(n,p)$ with auxiliary randomness independent of $G$,
--
--   $$\mathbb P\Big[|M_{\mathrm{out}}|\le\Big(\tfrac12+\varepsilon\Big)L\Big]\ge1-\exp\Big(-\frac{\varepsilon^2}{2}L\log(np)\Big).$$
--
--   The probability is over the graph and the algorithm's randomness. The algorithm may choose its inspection order adaptively and has unbounded memory and computation. Since Greedy is such an algorithm, Theorem 2.1 is optimal.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: vertex set [n] = {1, …, n} → Fin n (0-based); edges are Sym2 (Fin n) in G.edgeSet, matchings Finset (Sym2 (Fin n)); reason: relabelling of the vertices; same instances
--   - encoding: G ∼ G(n, p), each pair an edge independently with probability p → the published law LPCommunity.SBMRecovery.erProb n p (edge weights clamp01 p); reason: clamp01 is the identity on [0, 1], which contains every p of the statement
--   - encoding: Definition 2.2: choices are functions of previous choices and revealed adjacencies → rules next/accept of (G, S, M) with locality fields; an inspected vertex chosen means termination, an illegal partner means no acceptance; reason: every algorithm of Definition 2.2 is such a structure and every such structure runs as one
--   - generalization: auxiliary randomness independent of the graph, every choice a measurable function of the history and the randomness → any probability space (Ω, μ) and A : Ω → OnlineAlg n with every event {ξ : output (A ξ) G = M} measurable; probability Σ_G P[G] · μ{ξ : …}; reason: an algorithm of Definition 2.2 with seed ξ gives such a family (measurable choices make the output measurable); only output measurability is required, so the class is at least the source's
--   - encoding: the seed's probability space, in any universe → Ω : Type (universe 0); reason: a theorem cannot quantify over universes inside its statement; pushing the seed forward to OnlineAlg n (a Type, with the σ-algebra generated by the output events) gives an algorithm in this form with the same probabilities
-- source:
--   Dhawan, Kızıldağ, Maitra, Mao, Wee, Half-Optimal Induced Matchings in Random Graphs: Online and Low-Degree Algorithms, arXiv:2610.40369 (held version, sha256 a1b329a1…), Theorem 2.3, p.10 (PDF p.12)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LPCommunity_SBMRecovery_Setting
import Definitions.Def_HalfIndMatch_Lval
import Definitions.Def_HalfIndMatch_OnlineAlg

open LPCommunity.SBMRecovery MeasureTheory

namespace HalfIndMatch

/-- Theorem 2.3, p.10: fix `κ ∈ (0, 1)` and `ε > 0`. There is `C = C(ε, κ) > 0` such that, for all
sufficiently large `n`, all `C/n ≤ p ≤ 1 − κ`, and every online algorithm as in Definition 2.2
(with arbitrary auxiliary randomness independent of the graph),
`P[|M_out| ≤ (1/2 + ε)L] ≥ 1 − exp(−(ε²/2) L log(np))`.

Source: Dhawan, Kızıldağ, Maitra, Mao, Wee, *Half-Optimal Induced Matchings in Random Graphs: Online and Low-Degree Algorithms*, arXiv:2610.40369 (held version, sha256 a1b329a1…), Theorem 2.3, p.10 (PDF p.12).

**Formalization Note** A randomized algorithm is a probability space `(Ω, μ)` (the auxiliary seed)
and a map `A : Ω → OnlineAlg n` with measurable output events; the probability is over the product
of `G ∼ G(n, p)` and an independent seed `ξ ∼ μ`, written `Σ_G P[G] · μ{ξ : …}`. A seed fixed at the
start covers randomness used over time. -/
theorem theorem_2_3 (κ ε : ℝ) (hκ0 : 0 < κ) (hκ1 : κ < 1) (hε0 : 0 < ε) :
    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧ ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ n : ℕ, N ≤ n → ∀ p : ℝ, C / n ≤ p → p ≤ 1 - κ →
      ∀ (Ω : Type) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
        (A : Ω → OnlineAlg n),
        (∀ (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (M : Finset (Sym2 (Fin n))),
          MeasurableSet {ξ : Ω | output (A ξ) G = M}) →
        1 - Real.exp (-(ε ^ 2 / 2 * Lval n p * Real.log ((n : ℝ) * p))) ≤
          ∑ G : SimpleGraph (Fin n), edgeWeight G (fun _ _ => p) *
            μ.real {ξ : Ω | ((output (A ξ) G).card : ℝ) ≤ (1 / 2 + ε) * Lval n p} := by sorry

end HalfIndMatch
